# Firebase client configuration. Not a credential — it holds no private key and
# grants no authority — but it names the Firebase project and its API key to
# anyone who clones a public repo, so it is provisioned locally instead. The
# Android build fails without it; apps/mobile/README.md says where to put it.
google-services.json

# Firebase service-account keys — the opposite of the file above. This one holds
# a real private key with admin authority over the entire Firebase project, so a
# single accidental commit to a public repo hands that project away. It belongs
# on the API host as FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_BASE64 and nowhere in this tree.
# The patterns match the console's own download name, because that name is what
# actually lands in a working directory, and they are deliberately broad: the
# cost of ignoring an unrelated file is a puzzled minute, the cost of missing one
# of these is the project.
*firebase-adminsdk*.json
*service-account*.json
*serviceaccount*.json

# Test and tool reports
test-results/
reports/
